              "title": "Why we forget",
              "difficulty": null,
              "timeLimitMinutes": null,
              "audioRef": null,
              "transcriptRef": null,
              "passage": "<p><strong>A</strong> Most people assume that forgetting is a failure of memory. Researchers now think it is often useful: by letting go of unimportant details, the brain keeps the important ones easy to find.</p><p><strong>B</strong> In the 1880s the German psychologist Hermann Ebbinghaus tested himself with lists of nonsense syllables. He found that most of what he learned disappeared within a day, and then the loss slowed down.</p><p><strong>C</strong> Sleep plays a large part in deciding what stays. During deep sleep the brain replays the day's experiences and strengthens the links that matter.</p><p><strong>D</strong> Stress has the opposite effect. Students who revise while anxious often remember less, because stress hormones make it harder to store new information.</p><p><strong>E</strong> The good news is that memory can be trained. Testing yourself, rather than simply re-reading notes, is one of the most reliable ways to remember more.</p><p><strong>F</strong> Finally, forgetting can protect us. People who keep every painful detail of the past find it harder to move on, so a little forgetting may support mental health.</p>",
