              "title": "The return of the bicycle",
              "difficulty": null,
              "timeLimitMinutes": null,
              "audioRef": null,
              "transcriptRef": null,
              "passage": "<p>Fifty years ago the bicycle seemed to belong to the past. Cars were becoming cheaper, cities were building wider roads, and cycling was something children did in the park. Today, many cities are trying hard to bring bicycles back.</p><p>The reasons are partly practical. In crowded centres a bicycle is often the fastest way to cross town, and it needs very little space to park. Cities also want cleaner air, and every short trip made by bicycle instead of by car reduces pollution.</p><p>Copenhagen is often given as an example. There, more than forty per cent of people travel to work or school by bicycle. This did not happen by accident: the city spent decades building separate lanes protected from traffic.</p><p>Not everyone is convinced. Some shop owners fear that removing car parking will cost them customers, although studies in several cities found that cyclists visit local shops more often than drivers. Others point out that cycling is difficult in hot or very hilly places.</p><p>Electric bicycles may solve some of these problems. They make hills and long distances easier, and they are popular with older riders. Whether they will change transport in the way supporters hope is still uncertain.</p>",
