              "title": "The Aral Sea",
              "difficulty": null,
              "timeLimitMinutes": null,
              "audioRef": null,
              "transcriptRef": null,
              "passage": "<p>The Aral Sea was once the fourth largest lake in the world. From the 1960s, the rivers that fed it, the Amu Darya and the Syr Darya, were used to water huge cotton fields. As less water reached the sea, it began to shrink.</p><p>By the early 2000s the sea had split into separate parts, and fishing towns such as Moynaq found themselves many kilometres from the water. Salt and dust from the dry seabed were carried by the wind, harming crops and people's health.</p><p>Since then, efforts have been made to limit the damage. A dam completed in 2005 helped the northern part of the sea to recover, and fish have returned there. In the south, millions of salt-tolerant plants have been planted on the dry seabed to stop the dust.</p><p>Scientists say the sea will never return to its old size, but careful use of water could still improve life for the people who live around it.</p>",
